=================================== FINDINGS ===================================

A7 (advisory, NEW) — behaviour 11's new verify row has no test-validity guard: it is vacuously true when its own
  fixture breaks. Measured: repointing the child at "verify-NOPE.ts" (M17) leaves the whole file GREEN, because the
  block's `catch` assigns `out = { exitCode: -1, stderr: String(err) }` and the predicate
  `!out.stderr.includes("working tree is dirty")` accepts that. A rename or move of verify.ts, or any tsx failure,
  silently turns the row into a no-op and M15 becomes re-survivable with nothing going red.
  ONE INSTANCE. Searched: every negation- or absence-shaped `check()` predicate in scripts/no-vendor.test.mjs
  (13 of them: :131, :223, :286, :292, :350, :607, :693, :720, :727, :740, :774, :801, :943). This is the only one
  whose enclosing block swallows its own fixture's failure into a value the predicate accepts. The others are each
  either self-validating (a throw propagates) or paired in-block with a positive assertion that fails on a broken
  fixture — behaviour 7's sibling tsx child has the identical catch shape but consults the parsed value
  (`harnessEnvOut.PATH === "x"`), so a broken child fails it; and behaviour 9 row 5 carries an explicit
  "the throw path actually threw (test validity)" check for exactly this reason.
  NOT blocking: the mechanism IS pinned now and the assertion demonstrably kills M15 today; what is missing is the
  guard that keeps the pin honest, one layer out. Remedy is one clause the row's own detail string already prints:
  `out.exitCode === 0 && !out.stderr.includes("working tree is dirty")`. The house has a precedent for it two
  behaviours up, which is why it belongs on the record rather than in silence.

Carried from round 1, unchanged and still advisory (none was a condition of passing):
